kolt/src/storage.vl:6-41 hand-writes the whole surface, five FIXMEs deep:

    // FIXME: Implement with std.
    external struct Storage {}

    impl Storage {
        // FIXME: Implement with std.
        [extern(get, "length")]  external fun len(self): u53;
        // FIXME: Implement with std.
        [extern(method, "key")]  external fun key_at(self, index: u53): str;
        // FIXME: Should return `Option<str>` based on whether the key exists or not.
        fun get(self, key: str): str { storage::get(key) }
        // FIXME: Implement with std.
        fun has(self, key: str): bool { !self.get(key).is_empty() }
        fun set(self, key: str, value: str) { storage::set(key, value) }
        fun remove(self, key: str) { storage::remove(key) }
    }

    impl Window {
        // FIXME: Implement with std.
        [extern(get, "localStorage")]  external fun local_storage(self): Storage;
    }

WHAT STD HAS TODAY: vilan/std/src/browser/storage.vl:1-24 is six FREE functions over the two host
objects — `get`/`set`/`remove` (:6-14) and `session_get`/`session_set`/`session_remove` (:16-24) — and
the module header states the shape: "A missing key reads as "" (the host returns null; the helper
flattens it)". There is no handle type, no length, no key enumeration, no presence test, and no way to
tell a stored `""` from an absent key.

Those last three are not hypothetical. kolt's `StorageKeyAllocator::seal` (storage.vl:79-102) sweeps the
store: `delete_unknown_keys` walks `len()` down to 0 calling `key_at(i - 1)` to delete every key the app
no longer declares, and `initialize_unset_keys` needs `has(key)` — which it cannot write correctly today,
because `!get(key).is_empty()` is false for a key whose stored value is legitimately `""`.

PROPOSED: a `Storage` handle reached as `window().local_storage()` and `window().session_storage()`, with
`len()`, `key_at(index): Option<str>`, `get(key): Option<str>`, `has(key): bool`, `set`, `remove`,
`clear()`, and an iterator over the keys. Keep the six free functions as the one-liner form; the flattening
`""` belongs only there, and the handle's `get` is the one that tells absent from empty.

TWO NOTES THE SWEEP COLLECTED ON THE SAME PATH, NEITHER FILED SEPARATELY:

- prefs.vl:27 `// TODO: Consider using the codec system to encode and decode.` is ALREADY answerable with
  shipped std. `std::json` carries per-value STRING codecs: `trait Json { fun to_json(self): str }`
  (json.vl:18-19) and `trait FromJson { fun from_json(text: str): Result<Self, str> }` (json.vl:148-152),
  with impls for str, i32, u32, f64, bool, Option and List (json.vl:184-289) — that is a str codec for a
  scalar, exactly what a storage value needs — plus `encode_json`/`decode_json` (json.vl:707, :717) for any
  `Wire` type. `json_codec()` (json.vl:743) is the FRAME-level codec for the wire and is NOT the thing to
  reach for here. Whether kolt's `StorageKey<T>` should take a codec instead of its `encode`/`decode`
  closure pair is an app decision on top of that, not a std gap.
- prefs.vl:15 `// TODO: Consider better way to write this.` is about the six-fold
  `StorageKey`/`StorageKeyAllocator` boilerplate in kolt's own `Prefs::new` — app ergonomics, nothing owed
  by std beyond what this item proposes.

Three hand-written `Event` getters, in three files:

- kolt/src/input_system.vl:5-9
      impl Event {
          // FIXME: Implement with std.
          [extern(get, "code")]  external fun code(self): str;
      }
  The keyboard-shortcut matcher wants the PHYSICAL key — app_input_system.vl spells its bindings
  "KeyE", "KeyB", "KeyD" — which is `event.code`, not `event.key`. A layout-independent shortcut cannot be
  written with `key` alone.

- kolt/src/views.vl:31-34
      impl Event {
          // FIXME: Implement with std.
          [extern(get, "currentTarget")]  external fun current_target(self): Element;
      }

- kolt/src/overlay.vl:91-94, under the banner the owner wrote himself
  ("// DOM bindings `std::dom` lacks yet"):
      impl Event {
          [extern(get, "target")]  external fun target(self): Element;
      }

WHAT STD HAS TODAY: vilan/std/src/browser/dom.vl:193-245 — `prevent_default` (:197), `button` (:201),
`meta_key`/`ctrl_key`/`shift_key`/`alt_key` (:205-215), `key` (:218), `pointer_x`/`pointer_y` (:229-232),
and `target_value` (:244). That last one reaches THROUGH the target to read its `.value`, so the host
property is already on the binding's path — what is missing is the accessor that hands the element back.

PROPOSED: `code(self): str`, `target(self): Element` and `current_target(self): Element` on
`std::dom::Event`, sited beside `key`. Keeping `target` and `current_target` distinct is the point, and
kolt's two uses are the exhibit: overlay.vl asks "did this click land inside me" (`target`, paired with
`Element::contains` — A59), views.vl asks "which element carries the handler that fired" (`current_target`).
Document `key` vs `code` in the same pass — a shortcut table wants `code`, a text-entry handler wants `key`,
and today's surface silently offers only the second.

kolt/src/overlay.vl:53-125 is twenty hand-written extern declarations under a banner the owner wrote:

    // ---------------------------------
    // DOM bindings `std::dom` lacks yet
    // ---------------------------------

    external struct DomRect;                          // left, top, width, height (:55-69)
    impl Element {
        [extern(method, "getBoundingClientRect")]  external fun bounding_rect(self): DomRect;
        [extern(method, "contains")]               external fun contains(self, other: Element): bool;
        [extern(get, "offsetWidth")]               external fun offset_width(self): f64;
        [extern(get, "offsetHeight")]              external fun offset_height(self): f64;
        [extern(get, "isConnected")]               external fun is_connected(self): bool;
    }
    impl Window {
        [extern(method, "addEventListener", retains)]
        external fun on_event_capture(self, event: str, handler: |Event| void, capture: bool): void;
        [extern(method, "removeEventListener")]
        external fun off_event_capture(self, event: str, handler: |Event| void, capture: bool): void;
        [must_use]
        fun listen_capture(self, event: str, handler: |Event| void): Subscription { ... }
    }
    external struct ResizeObserver;                   // new(callback), observe(target), disconnect() (:115-126)

plus kolt/src/views.vl:378-382:

    impl Element {
        /// Removes an attribute (`element.removeAttribute(name)`).
        [extern(method, "removeAttribute")]  external fun remove_attribute(self, name: str): void;
    }

WHAT STD HAS TODAY: vilan/std/src/browser/dom.vl — `set_text` (:47), `set_class` (:51), `set_attribute`
(:55-56), `set_style_property` (:61-62), `append`/`remove`/`clear` (:65-79), `set_hidden` (:82-83), `focus`
(:89-90), `value`/`set_value` (:94-99), `on`/`on_event`/`off_event` and the `[must_use]` `listen` wrapper
(:102-132) on Element, the same listener trio plus `listen` (:154-171) on Window. Nothing measures, nothing
observes, nothing removes an attribute, and both `listen` wrappers hard-code the BUBBLE phase.

Each binding has a named customer in kolt's own anchored-overlay path: it measures the anchor
(`bounding_rect`) and itself (`offset_width`/`offset_height`); it waits on `is_connected` before
positioning, because a detached element measures 0x0; it asks `contains` whether an outside click should
dismiss it; it listens for `scroll` in the CAPTURE phase because, in the owner's own comment, that is "the
only way to hear `scroll` from every scrolling ancestor at once, which is what keeps an anchored menu on its
element while the page scrolls under it"; and it re-positions on `ResizeObserver`. views.vl's
`remove_attribute` is the missing half of `set_attribute` — its `toggle_attr` (views.vl:384-396) sets the
attribute to `""` when true and must delete it when false, which today's surface cannot express.

PROPOSED, in rising order of how much design each needs:
1. `Element::remove_attribute` beside `set_attribute`. An omission, not a design question.
2. `listen_capture` beside `listen` on Element and Window, same `[must_use]` `Subscription` contract.
3. A measurement surface: `DomRect` plus `Element::bounding_rect`, `offset_width`, `offset_height`,
   `is_connected`, `contains`.
4. Resize observation. Prefer the wrapper kolt built over the raw class — `observe_resize(owner, target,
   on_resize)` (overlay.vl:128) fires once on first render and disconnects when the owner disposes, which is
   the shape an app wants; the bare `ResizeObserver` is a `retains` callback that leaks unless every caller
   remembers to disconnect it.

kolt/src/routes.vl:22-52, on a route enum written the way the router paper wants (the file's header calls
the URL "a wire format for this enum"):

    // FIXME: Use path helpers and add url parsing helpers to `std::router`. Or, establish
    //        a canonical url parsing format that's easier to read and write than the below.
    fun parse(path: str): Route {
        let parts = segments(path);
        match parts.len() {
            0 => Route::Home,
            _ => {
                if parts[0] == "m" {
                    Route::Messages
                } else if parts[0] == "w" && parts.len() > 1 {
                    match parts[1].parse_i32() {
                        Some(let id) => Route::Workspace(id, parse_workspace(parts)),
                        None => Route::NotFound,
                    }
                } else {
                    Route::NotFound
                }
            },
        }
    }

    fun parse_workspace(parts: List<str>): WorkspaceRoute {
        if parts.len() > 3 && parts[2] == "task" {
            match parts[3].parse_i32() {
                Some(let task_id) => WorkspaceRoute::Task(task_id),
                None => WorkspaceRoute::Overview,
            }
        } else {
            WorkspaceRoute::Overview
        }
    }

WHAT STD HAS TODAY: vilan/std/src/browser/router.vl ships the WRITE direction and all the plumbing —
`trait Routable { fun to_path(self): str }` (:81-83), `link` (:89), `navigate` (:60), `current_path` (:52),
`history_push` (:23), `plain_left_click` (:101), `pending` (:114), `chunk_error` (:124) — and exactly ONE
read helper: `segments(path): List<str>` (:69). Everything above `segments` is the app's, and the index
arithmetic is where a route bug will live: `parts.len() > 3 && parts[2] == "task"` then `parts[3]` reads
like an off-by-one even when it is right.

Note also that no query string or fragment is reachable at all — `location_path()` (:27) is the path only,
so an app that wants `?q=` has to go around std to `window.location`.

THE ASYMMETRY IS THE ITEM: `Routable` makes a route's URL form one declaration in the write direction and
there is no counterpart for reading, so the enum's wire format is stated twice, in two shapes, and can
drift.

THREE CANDIDATE ANSWERS FOR THE OWNER TO CHOOSE BETWEEN (they compose; (c) is needed whichever wins):
(a) matcher helpers over `segments` — a `Segments` cursor with `take(literal): bool`,
    `take_int(): Option<i32>`, `rest()`, so the shape above reads top-down with no indices;
(b) a `FromPath` counterpart to `Routable`, making the enum's URL wire format one declaration in both
    directions (this is what "a canonical url parsing format" most plausibly means, and it is the shape
    that makes the paper's "URL is a wire format for this enum" literally true);
(c) query and fragment access — `location_query()` and a parsed `Query`, plus percent-decoding — which is
    missing outright.

kolt/src/debounce.vl is 37 lines of app code that is not app-specific:

    struct Debounce {
        delay: Duration,
        // TODO: Investigate whether the `Shared` cell is needed.
        runner: Shared<Option<(Instant, || void)>>,
    }

    impl Debounce {
        fun run(self, fn: || void) {
            if self.runner.read() is Some(mut runner) {
                runner.0 = now().add(self.delay);
                runner.1 = fn;
            } else {
                self.runner.write() = Some((now().add(self.delay), fn));
                async {
                    for self.runner.read() is Some(let (moment, _)) {
                        let timer = Timer::after_for(moment.since(now()));
                        if !timer.wait() { jump break; }
                        let (moment, fn) = self.runner.read().unwrap();
                        if now() < moment { jump continue; }
                        fn();
                        self.runner.write() = None;
                    }
                };
            }
        }
    }

WHAT STD HAS TODAY: `std::time` ships the primitives and nothing built from them — `Timer::after` /
`after_for` / `wait` / `cancel` (time.vl:238-271), `sleep` / `sleep_for` (:191, :197), `Instant` and
`Duration` with the arithmetic this needs (:33-109). No debounce, no throttle. A tracker grep finds
`debounce` only in E121 (the LSP's own diagnostics debounce — a NUMBER, "clamp(0.3 x last analysis, 150,
500)", not a mechanism) and in A14, where the draft-reconnect ruling says "no default debounce" — which is
about std declining to debounce ON THE APP'S BEHALF, a different question from std offering the tool.

The semantics kolt landed on are the ones worth standardising, and they are subtler than a `sleep`: one
in-flight timer ever; each `run` pushes the deadline out AND replaces the callback; the loop re-reads the
deadline after every wait, so a call that arrived during a wait cannot fire early. It runs the LAST
callback, not the first — trailing edge.

THE OWNER'S OWN TODO, ANSWERED: YES, the `Shared` cell is required, and rule 1 is why. `run` takes `self` by
loan; the `async` block OUTLIVES that loan — it reads `self.runner` on every timer tick, long after `run`
returned — so a plain field would be a read through a dead loan. `Shared` is the cell that makes the escape
legal, and rule 3's interior mutability is what lets the block WRITE it (`self.runner.write() = None`)
through what is only a loan. Both directions are load-bearing; it cannot be a plain field. (The same
read-then-write-the-same-cell shape is what B267's cell-aware last-use elision reasons about, so this is a
customer for that work too.)

PROPOSED: `std::time::Debounce` with `new(delay)`, `run(fn)`, `cancel()` and `flush()` (fire the pending
callback now), the trailing-edge semantics above stated in the doc rather than left to be discovered, and
kolt's implementation taken as the starting point with its `Shared` cell kept. Rule a `Throttle` sibling in
or out in the same pass — leading-edge is the other half of the question and it will be asked next.
